# Documentation Maintenance

When you add, change, or remove a feature, **always update documentation as part of the same task**.

## What to Update

### CHANGELOG.md (required for every feature/fix)
Add an entry under the current unreleased version (or create a new version block).
- New features → `### ✨ New Features`
- Enhancements → `### ✨ Enhanced Features`
- Bug fixes → `### 🐛 Bug Fixes`
- DB changes → `### 🗄️ Database Changes`
- API changes → note under the feature description

### .cursor/rules/*.mdc (required when changing backend logic)
Update the relevant rule file so future AI agents have accurate context:
- Changed filter params or sort fields → update `dashboard-search.mdc`
- Changed generation options, answer modes, or doc structure → update `document-generation.mdc`
- Changed admin routes or ingestion logic → update `admin-panel.mdc`
- Changed models → update `data-models.mdc`
- Changed filename patterns or ingestor logic → update `file-ingestion.mdc`
- New blueprint or major new feature area → add a new `.mdc` rule file

### DEVELOPER_SPEC.md (update for API/schema/architecture changes)
Keep the API endpoint tables, DB schema, and architecture sections current.

### README.md (update for feature additions visible at a glance)
Update the Features section and project structure if it changes significantly.

### USER_MANUAL.md (update for any user-facing behaviour changes)
Keep the filter, generation, and UI descriptions accurate.

### ADMIN_GUIDE.md (update for admin-panel or deployment changes)
Keep admin operations and configuration instructions current.

## When to Skip

- Pure refactors with no behaviour change → update code comments only
- Styling/layout-only changes → no doc update needed
- Typo/small fixes → no doc update needed

## Rule File Format Reminder

```
---
description: One-line description
globs: app/somefile.py,templates/some*.html   # omit for alwaysApply rules
alwaysApply: false                            # true for global rules
---
```

Keep rule files **concise** (under 150 lines). They are loaded into every relevant chat — brevity matters.
